the right man in the wrong place can  make all the difference in the world so  wake up mr. Freeman wake up and smell  the ashes it's hard to predict the  impact a game might have long after its  release these things need time to  percolate to sink in to become part of  the mediums culture we need to be able  to look back and see them in the grand  scheme of things but in the case of  half-life 2 in 2004 it seemed obvious  right away this game would change things  and it did even if you still haven't  played it you've seen its influence  everywhere half-life 2 set precedents  for the way games can tell stories both  in the first-person shooter genre and  elsewhere it showed how physics pacing  and a cohesive world can all combine to  form a narrative epic many games would  not exist today at Valve's sequel hadn't  first blazed the trail Bioshock Dead  Space  The Last of Us and Wolfenstein the new  order just to name a few but we also  wouldn't have the metro series from for  a games and in the wake of Microsoft's  e3 2017 press conference where we got  our first glimpse at Metro Exodus the  third game in that series I want to turn  your attention to that game's  predecessor Metro last light the 2013  title is a fluid blend of horror and  survival set within the decrepit tunnels  of post-apocalyptic Russia it's also  criminally underappreciated and one of  the most brilliant shooters ever made  but in a wider sense it did something  even more important it came along at a  time when story driven first-person  shooters were few and far between it  also borrowed from the best shooter ever  made continuing half-life 2's legacy and  setting new precedents of its own the  Metro is a living breathing thing  with a heartbeat a soul  [Music]  to understand the significance of both  titles its first important to understand  the climate in which they released in  2004 half-life 2 launched within weeks  of halo 2 grand theft auto san andreas  world of warcraft and the original Star  Wars Battlefront that holiday season was  packed with household franchise names at  a time when the medium was showing a  major focus on multiplayer titles  despite the anticipation spawned from  the original half-life its sequel  released at a more than difficult time  nine years later last light did the same  thing 2013 saw the release of another  new Grand Theft Auto a battlefield and  Assassin's Creed and of course a Call of  Duty games whose names alone guaranteed  sales despite their sometimes evident  fatigue this was also the year of  BioShock Infinite and the Last of Us two  games that told sweeping stories in a  dystopian society and post-apocalyptic  America the Last of Us did feature a  multiplayer component but in general  these releases were largely geared  toward those who craved a single-player  narrative with the pedigree of  Irrational Games and Naughty Dog however  they had no problem finding an audience  last light on the other hand didn't have  the same advantages it was a sequel to  Metro 2033 a cult classic based on the  series of novels by Russian writer  Dmitry Glukhovsky its developer for a  games was largely unknown as well  what's more the bankruptcy of publisher  THQ forced the developer to find a new  home midway through last light's  development yet somehow this didn't  deter 4a games from making a wholly  original atmospheric shooter like  half-life 2 it tells its story in novel  confident ways in a setting that seeps  into every mechanic and system at play  valve sequel was famous for its lack of  cutscenes instead opting to keep the  player rooted in protagonist gordon  freeman with total first-person control  over his movement and eyeline NPCs  delivered their dialogue as you move  throughout the environment creating a  sense of presence that a cutscene  couldn't all in all there's an  astonishing lack of exposition in  half-life 2 it tells its tale through  environmental cues character quirks and  the numerous ways you can manipulate the  world Last Light does this as well its  environments foreshadow the threats  you'll soon be facing its characters  accompany you at different times  throughout the story changing the  context of your actions and your  motivations for doing so  last life does have cutscenes but most  of them are brief or used to illustrate  a point  for instance the camera shifts to third  person when protagonist Artyom saves the  last dark one it's a method to emphasize  a similarity between their species  outside of these rare shifts though last  light makes a clear effort to keep you  rooted in Artyom shoes which is all to  say that neither half-life 2 nor last  light erode their narratives with  heavy-handed explanations they drop you  in forcing you to make sense of the  world as you go the settlements in  half-life's European dystopia feel  established as do the Stations of metros  Russian underground neither valve nor 4a  explicitly tell us that the characters  are accustomed to these ways of life  they show us perhaps most importantly  these are shooters in which the shooting  itself is not actually the main focus  it's the world building that's key the  shooting just helped to reinforce it the  most notable example is half-life 2's  iconic gravity gun not only does it let  you manipulate your surroundings and the  game's lauded physics engine but it also  makes sense both for your character and  within the game's world it's the kind of  makeshift weapon a scientific everyman  might theoretically use if he were  rising up against an oppressive military  regime less lights items guns and tools  take this contextual realism to its  extreme you manually pump pneumatic  weapons constructed from the scavenge  remains of the russian wasteland enemy  outfits are Frankenstein's monsters of  sheet metal dirty rags and hazmat suits  couple all of this with last night's  survival systems and the  post-apocalyptic atmosphere becomes even  more palpable assuming you're playing  less light on Ranger difficulty which in  my opinion is the only way to play this  game you'll have a limited HUD and  scarce or supplies you've glanced at  your guns magazine to see how many  rounds you have left you check your  watch to see the time remaining before  your suffocation when your gas mask  begins to spiderweb across your screen  you have to search high and low for a  replacement and of course your  high-grade ammunition doubles as  currency in a world where paper money  and coins are all but gone you're forced  to spend actual bullets at markets and  kiosks similar to bottle caps in the  Fallout games met Rosie  economy is shaped by what the people  deem valuable in this world in this case  bullets to defend themselves with  many of these things aren't necessarily  fun in the traditional sense in any  other game pumping your weapon charging  your flashlights battery or swapping  currency into and out of your magazine  could feel tedious but in last light  they contribute to the makeshift whims  of the metros inhabitants they make you  feel not like a soldier but like a  survivor like half-life 2's gravity gun  they ground you in this different world  the influences are here on a wire scale  as well much can be said about half-life  2's pacing and the way it propels you  from set pieces to set piece crafting  sandboxes and linear segments of light  it expertly handles the highs and lows  of a lengthy shooter campaign and last  slight follow suit in fact if you pay  close attention you'll notice how  similar the structures of the two games  really are you begin at a dystopian  settlement you're called away on a  mission a vehicle section emphasizes the  scope of your quest you venture into a  sinister area with otherworldly  creatures you actually cross a dangerous  bridge signaling your point of no return  you ally with those that were previously  your enemies you reach the end of your  journey where everything culminates in a  heated battle which is all to say that  last light touches on similar story  beats that half life 2 did emphasizing  the hero's journey that takes place from  beginning to end it understands what  made its forebear seminal to begin with  and warps it to fit a new world in a new  era for the medium we have rates and  d'afrique all caught in the same net I  do not like it so talk or take a bullet  to the brain  [Music]  [Music]  now Metro last light is not perfect it's  a I could have used some work and I  still can't stand the journal entries  spoken by the otherwise silent Artyom in  between chapters but last light realizes  how powerful the first-person shooter  genre can really be it builds a world  and crafts an atmosphere that leaves a  lasting impression everything else is in  service to that world building Metro  2033 may have been a cult classic and  last light may still be underappreciated  but with Metro Exodus on the horizon I  think it's time that more people  experienced forays phenomenal shooters  we probably won't see half-life's next  episode anytime soon but with games like  Last Light continuing the legacy of  Valve's legendary sequel it's hard to  care too much I can rest assured that  narrative driven first-person shooters  will always find an audience and they'll  always attract the creative minds to  make them so here's hoping that Metro  Exodus continues that tradition  [Music]
 

